# ============================================================================
# orca-serve.conf.template — SHARED HOST configuration (global, all instances inherit)
#
# Deployed to @PREFIX@/etc/orca-serve.conf and loaded by orca-serve@.service as
# the FIRST EnvironmentFile= line (before the per-instance instances/<instance>.env,
# which may override any value here).
#
# Authoring rule: a value goes HERE if it could ever differ by HOST. A value that
# differs by INSTANCE goes in etc/instances/<instance>.env instead. Never duplicate
# a value in both places. Every value below is a real Orca variable, not a wrapper
# knob — editing + redeploying this file must never require a unit rebuild.
#
# Copy to orca-serve.conf (drop the .template suffix) and set per-host values.
# ============================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Host identity
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The Orca environment id this host serves. Must exactly match a `name` in the
# environment registry; clients select it with `orca --environment <id>` (or the
# ORCA_ENVIRONMENT ambient variable). This is the value that makes
# "Unknown environment: <id>" resolvable on the client side.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ORCA_ENVIRONMENT=default

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything else is flags, not env.
#
# Upstream `orca serve` takes the values that used to be host/env knobs on its
# command line, so the unit carries them in ExecStart= (substituted at install):
#
#   --port <port>                 per-instance listener pin (instances/<instance>)
#   --pairing-address <addr>      client-advertised endpoint (per-host)
#
# The installer substitutes @PORT@ and @PAIRING_ADDRESS@ from its --port /
# --pairing-address flags into the rendered unit. Everything else the old wrappers
# configured as environment is a flag upstream — keep only real Orca variables in
# this file.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
